uation where ClojureScript's core `seq` function has been called on an object that does not implement the necessary protocols to be treated as a sequence [1]. In ClojureScript, for an object to be treated as a sequence, it must implement the `ISeqable` protocol [2], which provides a `-seq` method that returns an implementation of the `ISeq` protocol [3]. If you are seeing an error related to missing sequence support, it is likely because you are passing an unsupported type to a function that expects a sequence (such as `map`, `first`, or `rest`). To resolve this, you have a few options depending on your goal: 1. Use `cljs.core/seq` on compatible types: The built-in `seq` function handles collections (like vectors, lists, maps, and sets), strings, and JavaScript objects that satisfy the JavaScript iteration protocol [1]. If you have a custom JavaScript object, ensure it is iterable or implements `ISeqable`. 2. Use `lazy-seq`: If you are trying to create a custom sequence structure, the standard way to do this is using the `lazy-seq` macro [4]. This macro handles the complexity of caching and protocol implementation for you [4]. 3. Implement `ISeqable` using `reify` or `deftype`: If you need to make a custom object sequenceable, you can implement the `ISeqable` protocol [2]. You can use `reify` for a one-off instance [5]: (seq (reify ISeqable (-seq [this];; Return an ISeq or nil if empty (list 1 2 3)))) If you are seeing this error from a specific library or function, ensure the data you are passing to it is a standard ClojureScript collection or a JS array/string, as these are natively supported by `seq` [1].\n\nCitations:\n\n- 1: https://cljs.github.io/api/cljs.core/seq\n- 2: https://cljs.github.io/api/cljs.core/ISeqable\n- 3: https://cljs.github.io/api/cljs.core/ISeq\n- 4: https://cljs.github.io/api/cljs.core/lazy-seq\n- 5: https://cljs.github.io/api/cljs.core/reify\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Return after a shape-validation failure.**\n\nIf `:catalog/lanes` is `